e it messy and any one of  them if it were the only thing you'd be  like yeah whatever you can adjust for  that but when you add like 20 of them  together all of a sudden you're like oh  god like the market is disagreeing with  me trading in international markets was  no doubt hard work but it taught sam how  to find opportunities with asymmetrical  risk these are situations where your  downside is 1x but your upside could be  unlimited and soon enough such an  opportunity presented itself to sam  [Music]  in 2017 he discovered that bitcoin was  trading at a premium on korean  cryptocurrency exchanges if the price of  bitcoin was ten thousand dollars on  coinbase it could be as much as fifteen  thousand in korea this happened so  frequently that traders started  referring to the phenomenon as the  kimchi premium in theory you could  arbitrage this buy bitcoin in the united  states transfer it to a korean exchange  sell it for korean wan transfer the  money out from korea back to the us and  make a 30 to 50 profit on each trade but  this strategy wouldn't scale because  korean capital control laws limit  foreign transactions over five thousand  dollars however sam found a similar  opportunity in japan the premiums were  lower only five to twenty percent above  u.s prices but this could still prove  immensely profitable sam knew that he  had to move fast together with gary wang  who had been sam's roommate in college  he founded a trading firm called alameda  research and raised 1 million dollars  from personal savings and close friends  but there were a bunch of hurdles to  overcome before alameda could take  advantage of the japanese arbitrage  opportunity exchanges were backlogged  and it took weeks for sam to get a  coinbase account and once he started  depositing money in coinbase his bank  shut down his account back then the  traditional financial sector wanted  nothing to do with cryptocurrency sam  also needed a bank account in japan so  we hired a lawyer to go to the bank and  explain what alameda was doing it didn't  go well though and then you're like all  right can we send this to the asset like  what are we doing with it uh it's crypto  trading to like can you repeat that what  sam was trying to do was a red flag for  any bank actually every single day i  would like to send like five million  dollars in the same direction it's only  going one way  to a different country  and it's a different entity this is the  sketchiest [ __ ] thing i've ever seen  alameda did manage to get a japanese  bank account but this was just the start  the price delta couldn't last forever so  alameda wanted to repeat the trade as  many times as possible before the  opportunity disappeared every day sam  and his team would execute these eight  steps one wire us dollars to coinbase  two buy some bitcoin transfer that  bitcoin to japan then sell the bitcoin  in japan for yen convert that yen to us  dollars finally wire those dollars back  to the u.s bank account it was a  complicated loop with zero margin for  air and it was made worse by the fact  that the bank had a terrible user  interface that would only let you  convert a hundred thousand dollars at a  time you know when we're at this like  converting step um and took a minute per  hundred thousand dollars so we just all  stopped doing we all broke out on our  computers they're just like clicking  through eventually like when we had like  one minute left we were like all right  guys it's time whatever that is that no  more sam would then notify an alameda  employee who was waiting at the bank in  japan that person would walk up to the  teller and say that they wanted to make  a wire transfer and like to wear and  like that all the details and like  watched i say typed in like the  reference you know looks like [ __ ]  reference numbers yeah if everyone's  like three q seven t is like no it's a g  not a t but you gotta cross that out to  g not a t and then eventually hopefully  they subm